-\section*{Multimodal Monitoring in Robotic Welding}
-\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Multimodal Monitoring in Robotic Welding}
-During robotic welding, a camera can observe changes in the weld pool while a microphone records the sound of the process. The two observations can respond differently to the same change. The main research question asks how to design and evaluate a process-aware architecture that uses such evidence to detect anomalies reliably. To judge the value of a second input, the joint model and its strongest single-input alternative must predict the same outcome on the same welds. Two primary welding studies illustrate why this comparison matters.
-
-The dynamic multimodal measurement fusion network for welding (DMAW) combined image and audio Transformer representations to classify four welding states. Accuracy reached 96.1\% with fusion, compared with 90.2\% for the image model and 84.4\% for the audio model. The reported evaluation leaves the grouping of windows by welding run and performance during a controlled outage unresolved \citep[PDF p.~7, Table~III]{P003}. In a weld-penetration study, the image-only, audio-only, and fused models each reached 100\% accuracy on the clean test set. The fused model achieved 93.33\% under the reported interference condition. Determining the benefit of fusion during interference requires the single-input models to be evaluated under the corresponding conditions \citep[PDF pp.~18, 22, Tables~1--3]{Hua2026Weld}. Together these studies establish that learned visual--acoustic fusion already exists in welding and that its observed advantage depends on the endpoint and comparator.
-
-The original proposal concerned Intel Welding. The subsequent audit documented previous use of the proposed test population, which prevented an independent weld-quality assessment (Chapter~\ref{ch:introduction}). The review therefore follows the design question into adjacent tasks. Industrial condition studies clarify how a physical measurement becomes a prediction target. Robotic and machining studies show what can be compared when a camera and machine signals observe the same operation. The four subquestions concern the representation of each input (SQ1), the value of combining inputs (SQ2), behavior when an input fails or degrades (SQ3), and the evaluation of uncertainty, decisions, and practical use (SQ4). Section~\ref{sec:literature-study-gaps} identifies the specific comparison each of the five implementations could make.
-
-\section{Review Scope and Search Methodology}
-
-The review traced industrial fusion claims to the methods, comparison tables, and results in the publications inspected. A gap here means a comparison that those results leave unresolved for a specified target, set of inputs, and assessment population. The search and access records limit how broadly that inference can be made.
-
-\subsection{Research Themes and Search Questions}
-
-The representation theme asks which observations are available, what transformations are applied, and whether a representation preserves information relevant to the target. The fusion theme asks whether additional inputs improve a common prediction task against credible single-input references. The robustness theme asks which absence, corruption, or distribution-change regime was actually evaluated. The evaluation theme examines independence, thresholds, uncertainty, resource measurement, and reproducibility. These questions deliberately include negative outcomes: a fusion mechanism should be informative even when an added modality provides little benefit.
-
-The review also distinguishes two objectives often grouped together. Learning with several modalities to deploy a single-input model is a privileged-training problem. Combining several live inputs during deployment is a multimodal-inference problem. Cross-modal distillation and reconstruction connect these objectives, but their acquisition assumptions differ. The taxonomy of \citet{Baltrusaitis2019Multimodal} provides an organizing vocabulary; industrial claims are appraised in the original studies rather than inferred from that survey.
-
-\subsection{Sources, Search Terms, and Search Period}
-The literature review developed throughout the project. One documented search round focused on work published in 2016--2026, with selected earlier methodological contributions. Of the 120 candidates screened in that round, 39 met the inclusion criteria. The corresponding full-text appraisal covered 37 of those works. The later expansion identified \ExpandedPDFCount{} distinct full-text files after reconciling duplicate downloads and checking work versions. The current comparison register comprises \ReviewWorkCount{} distinct work records, rather than an estimate of the size or prevalence of the entire field.
-
-Searches paired industrial monitoring and manufacturing terms with fusion stage, attention, missing inputs, noise, and domain shift. Dataset searches included MVTec AD, MVTec 3D-AD, Eyecandies, bearing datasets, and machine-sound benchmarks. Publisher and repository searches located publications. Crossref supplied metadata for identity checks \citep{Crossref2020REST}, and OpenAlex supported citation tracing \citep{Priem2022OpenAlex}. Zotero linked bibliographic records to the inspected PDFs and appraisal notes \citep{Zotero2026}. Appendix~\ref{app:literature} distinguishes the original screening population from the later full-text appraisal.
-
-\subsection{Inclusion and Exclusion Criteria}
-Direct industrial work concerns faults, condition, manufacturing quality, inspection or robotic task outcomes. Dataset papers and credible unimodal methods are also relevant because fusion needs a defined target and a strong reference. Transferable studies are labelled explicitly: optimization failures, missing-input mechanisms, uncertainty weighting and evaluation theory can inform industrial design without constituting industrial validation \citep{Wang2020What,P016,P023,Bengio2004CVVariance}.
-
-A PDF can remain in the register while serving only as background or carrying major methodological qualifications. Simulated robot regression, RGB--geometry inspection, physical camera--telemetry monitoring and one-camera feature fusion occupy different strata \citep{Altinses2023Fuzzy,P026,Thoduka2024Handover,P035}. Inaccessible evidence is not presumed irrelevant, and missing reporting is not proof that an operation was omitted. Author terminology alone does not determine whether a paper supplies multimodal acquisition, inference-time robustness or a fair unimodal comparison.
-
-\subsection{Evidence Extraction and Critical Appraisal}
-Each appraisal records source identity and read version, target and prediction unit, physical inputs, transformations, fusion computation, controls, partitioning, training access and reliability interventions. Results are linked to pages, tables or figures. Decisive PDF tables were checked where extraction could obscure input columns, values or footnotes. Full-text appraisal establishes inspection, not experimental reproduction or endorsement of every author conclusion.
-
-The comparison asks what was held constant and what changed. A common assessment cohort supports a pipeline comparison; it does not isolate fusion if encoders, supervision, augmentation or capacity also differ. Input-subset models, complete inference absence, corrupted present data and target-domain adaptation are coded separately. The comparison matrix in Appendix~\ref{app:literature}, Table~\ref{tab:literature-matrix}, supplies the work-level reference. Its compact codes require the accompanying protocol descriptions and source locators.
-
-\subsection{Limitations of the Review}
-The sources are neither exhaustive nor statistically independent. Many inspection papers reuse MVTec 3D-AD or Eyecandies, so repeated benchmark gains do not constitute repeated validation across factories. Preprints are version-specific and some publication details remain provisional. When few studies evaluated a condition, the review offers limited evidence about that condition.
-
-The primary MVTec 3D-AD table gives 249 normal and 948 anomalous tests, totaling 1,197; some later accounts give different counts \citep[Table~1]{Bergmann2022The}. The welding penetration paper has inconsistent acquisition/training counts, and a lightweight inspection paper gives different NwfDD values in its principal and ablation tables \citep[PDF pp.~12, 15]{Hua2026Weld}\citep[PDF p.~10, Tables~IV, VI, VII]{P035}. These problems reduce the precision of particular comparisons without invalidating every observation in a source.
-
-\section{Industrial Monitoring Tasks and Supervision}
-
-Different monitoring tasks impose different assumptions about training data, observations, and acceptable error. Their scores are therefore not interchangeable evidence for a general architecture. The target definition must precede the comparison of representation or fusion mechanisms.
-
-\subsection{Anomaly Detection, Defect Classification, and Failure Classification}
-Normal-only anomaly detection learns a nominal reference, whereas supervised defect classification uses labelled fault or quality classes. MVTec AD and MVTec 3D-AD exemplify the first setting; welding penetration recognition exemplifies the second \citep{Bergmann2019MVTec,Bergmann2022The,Hua2026Weld}. HANDOVER introduces a different endpoint: whether a completed interaction succeeded or failed through no approach, no grasp, no release or object drop \citep{Thoduka2024Handover}. A task failure need not denote physical damage or identify its cause.
-
-Supervision is consequently part of the comparison. The weakly supervised inspection study reintegrates MVTec data because training needs anomalous image-level labels, changing the ordinary normal-only contract \citep[PDF p.~8]{P035}. ZSG-IAD trains reports and masks before evaluating held-out categories or datasets; its zero-shot terminology concerns that boundary, rather than absence of anomaly-supervised training \citep[PDF pp.~6, 8--9]{P025}. Scores from these tasks cannot be combined as interchangeable evidence for a general architecture.
-
-\subsection{Condition Estimation and Regression}
-
-Continuous prediction requires attention to units, reference measurements, and the horizon at which a target is defined. Remaining useful life is not equivalent to an instantaneous defect probability. \citet{AlDulaimi2019A} evaluate a hybrid CNN--LSTM on simulated C-MAPSS trajectories using RMSE and an asymmetric prognostic score. Their two branches process the same selected telemetry channels; this is evidence about complementary representations of sensor data, not physical camera--sensor complementarity.
-
-The synthetic robot benchmarks of \citet{Altinses2025Performance} address cross-modal prediction, reconstruction, and downstream regression. Errors for different reconstructed outputs cannot automatically establish which physical modality is most useful for one common target. This distinction matters for proxy supervision: a system may predict a derived condition index accurately without demonstrating that the index measures deterioration. A defensible regression comparison specifies the target construction, scale, fitted preprocessing, and common evaluation cases before interpreting numerical improvement as better condition monitoring.
-
-\subsection{Dataset-Provided Labels, Derived Targets, and Proxy Supervision}
-
-Reference labels have different evidential roles. Post-build CT provides flaw localization for the voxel-level monitoring study of \citet{Petrich2021Multimodal}; microscopy and density measurements support quality classes in \citet{Li2022A}; operando X-ray imaging guides process-state labels in \citet{Pandiyan2022Deep}. The reference instrument may therefore supervise the model without being available at inference. Counting it as an input modality would overstate the deployed system's information.
-
-Derived labels require a further separation between prediction and independent validation. If a target is calculated from the same measurements supplied to the model, low prediction error may reflect recovery of that calculation rather than an independent physical state. Likewise, region-level annotations may assign a common quality class to many neighboring observations. These choices can be useful, but their spatial or temporal dependence affects the evaluation unit. The thesis consequently needs an explicit account of which targets are native annotations, independent measurements, or analytical proxies, and which conclusions each can support.
-
-\subsection{Retrospective Assessment and Online Monitoring}
-A predictor evaluated after a complete window is a retrospective assessor unless prediction-time availability is constrained. HANDOVER explicitly states that its baselines classify outcomes after the complete trial; causal online prediction remains future work \citep[PDF p.~7, Section~VI]{Thoduka2024Handover}. A post-pass photograph, layer summary and complete action therefore provide different information horizons from prospective monitoring \citep{P045,Petrich2021Multimodal}. This distinction does not invalidate offline classification; it limits the operational claim.
-
-Computation must also be separated from observation. DMAW uses twenty frames at 37 frames per second while reporting a shorter forward latency. Approximately half a second of observation is therefore distinct from computational delay \citep[PDF pp.~6--7]{P003}. Likewise, varying CNN--LSTM input duration does not by itself establish warning before a defect occurs \citep{Pandiyan2022Deep}. An online claim needs a causal information cutoff, acquisition delay and defined end-to-end workload.
-
-\section{Multimodal Data in Industrial Applications}
-
-Complementarity depends on independent observations, not simply on distinct tensor shapes. The review therefore distinguishes physical acquisition channels from derived views, and distinguishes ordinary pairing from the stronger correspondence required by cross-modal reconstruction or localized process monitoring.
-
-\subsection{Visual and Video Observations}
-RGB, thermal images, process videos and geometry have different acquisition assumptions. Depth, point clouds reconstructed from depth and estimated normals may be several representations of one geometric source. GPAD combines RGB--depth features with point-cloud priors; this does not establish three independent physical sensors \citep[PDF pp.~4--7]{P024}. RGB--geometry benchmarks describe appearance and shape, rather than controller dynamics or motor current \citep{Bergmann2022The,Bonfiglioli2023The}.
-
-Physical camera-plus-measurement precedents include thermal imaging with vibration, welding images with sound, tool photographs with acoustic emission and robot video with force--torque and gripper state \citep{Huo2022Multisensor,Hua2026Weld,P045,Thoduka2024Handover}. Their units range from static inspection to process windows and complete trials. The tool-wear camera is used after a cutting pass, whereas preceding acoustic emission describes active cutting \citep[PDF pp.~19--22]{P045}. A sequence of images can also be processed without learning temporal order: pooled frame features and a video Transformer are different representations even when both consume multiple frames.
-
-\subsection{Sensor Time Series and Process Measurements}
-
-Machine measurements vary in physical origin and semantic meaning. Accelerometers observe vibration, pressure sensors observe fluid behaviour, and controller logs may describe commands, estimates, or tracking errors rather than independently measured physical quantities. The harmonic-drive study combines high-frequency vibration with servo information, whereas PGMTKD transfers pressure--vibration teacher knowledge to a pressure-only student \citep{Yang2024Fault,Li2025PGMTKD}. Their sensor availability and deployment objectives differ despite both addressing missing information.
-
-Representations can further multiply apparent modalities without adding measurements. \citet{Kullu2022A} combine current and vibration with raw and STFT views. \citet{Wan2024Pipeline} derive statistical ``vibration'' features from two acoustic sensors; they do not acquire an accelerometer channel. \citet{Wang2025A} combine source-domain features and decisions from vibration data. These studies remain useful architectural comparators, but neither a second transform nor a second domain establishes an additional physical observation. Acquisition inventories must therefore precede counting modalities or attributing gains to sensor diversity.
-
-\subsection{Audio Observations}
-
-Audio can capture machine or process events that are weakly visible, but the mapping between recorded sound and fault state depends on background conditions, microphone placement, and synchronization. MIMII deliberately mixes factory background recordings at multiple SNR levels; its fan baseline AUC decreases from .94 at +6~dB to .70 at -6~dB \citep[Table~3]{Purohit2019MIMII}. ToyADMOS provides miniature-machine recordings and an explicit noise-mixing protocol, making it a useful acoustic reference rather than a demonstrated camera--sensor advantage \citep{Koizumi2019ToyADMOS}.
-
-In process monitoring, airborne acoustic signals may be paired with a melt-pool camera or a photodiode. The distinction is in the instruments. Signal-to-image encoding in \citet{Li2022A} preserves an acoustic--optical-signal experiment, whereas \citet{Xie2025Audiovisual} use actual camera observations. DCASE2020 Task2 provides audio-only baselines under an evaluation protocol that differs from those of later domain-shift challenges \citep{Koizumi2020Description}. Acoustic coverage in the review therefore motivates candidate representations without implying predictive audio evaluation in every thesis case study.
-
-\subsection{Synchronization, Correspondence, and Modality Availability}
-
-Fusion assumes that observations refer to a compatible event, region, or time interval. Stronger correspondence is required when a model predicts one modality from another or multiplies aligned discrepancy maps. RGB--point-cloud feature mapping exploits registered benchmark geometry; process fusion may instead need time synchronization and projection into a common spatial grid \citep{Costanzino2024Multimodal,Petrich2021Multimodal}. The digital-twin study registers sensor features to robot TCP positions, illustrating that position can support correspondence without serving as an ordinary predictive feature \citep{Chen2023Multisensor}.
-
-Availability has several levels: a file may exist, a sample may have a paired observation, and a usable signal may still be absent or degraded. None implies the others. Modality completeness should therefore be recorded per prediction unit with an explicit pairing rule. The acoustic--photodiode study explicitly lacks modality registration, which qualifies conclusions drawn from its encoded-window fusion \citep{Li2022A}. The same distinction matters for camera and machine-sensor data. A pairing record can identify unmatched events even when both files are present.
-
-\section{Representation Learning for Industrial Monitoring --- SQ1}
-
-SQ1 concerns the relationship between representations and task requirements. The evidence supports neither a universal preference for learned features nor an assumption that a large pretrained encoder is always necessary. Representation quality interacts with supervision, preprocessing, sample size, and the resources allocated to fitting.
-
-\subsection{Engineered Features and Conventional Machine Learning}
-Engineered statistics, spectral descriptors and conventional classifiers remain important references. Their relevance follows from predictive performance and information access, not from architectural novelty. Classical geometric features can be highly competitive for 3D inspection, and a multi-channel neural model can lose to a selected sensor \citep{Horwitz2023Back,Goodarzi2023Deep}. A strong reference prevents a weak learned branch from defining an artificially easy fusion comparison.
-
-Industrial vision also has lightweight alternatives. The weakly supervised inspection network uses one camera source, internal anomaly-aware features and a compact CNN; its reported cost is 2.33 million parameters and 5.65~ms per image \citep[PDF p.~10, Table~V]{P035}. This is relevant representation and cost evidence, but its anomalous training labels and modified MVTec supervision differ from normal-only inspection. Conventional and learned references should therefore be compared under the same target, assessment cases and fitted preprocessing rather than ranked by model family.
-
-\subsection{Pretrained Visual Representations}
-Pretrained CNN and Transformer features reduce industrial fitting requirements while importing substantial external learning. PatchCore, asymmetric student--teacher models and cross-modal feature mapping show useful nominal-data representations under different protocols \citep{Roth2022Towards,Rudolph2023Asymmetric,Costanzino2024Multimodal}. Their success supports transfer as a candidate strategy, not an architecture-independent guarantee.
-
-Vision-language inspection imports semantic context as well. DSE-AD combines historical/current rail images with normal/abnormal position prompts and evaluates pixel localization on another rail domain \citep{P031}. Its paired-reference and prompt assumptions differ from isolated image classification. Similarly, a detector checkpoint can supply only convolutional features: that use does not imply execution of a Transformer encoder, decoder or detection head. A representation description must identify the actual forward path, trainable components and observation horizon before an improvement can be attributed to pretraining or attention.
-
-\subsection{Temporal and Time-Series Representations}
-Temporal structure can be represented through statistics, spectra, recurrence, attention or aggregation. PatchTST tokenizes channels into patches and shares Transformer weights across channel-independent paths. Its supervised, masked-pretraining and transfer experiments are different fitting regimes \citep[PDF pp.~3--8]{Nie2023PatchTST}. On Weather horizon 96, the patch-plus-channel-independent model reports MSE .152 against .164 without patching; this supports the design within forecasting, not superiority for robotic failure classification \citep[PDF p.~9, Table~7]{Nie2023PatchTST}.
-
-Video-time attention addresses a different structure. TimeSformer separates temporal and spatial attention, while Video Swin introduces local spatiotemporal attention \citep{P034,P013}. Neither is equivalent to averaging independently encoded frames. The industrial CNN--LSTM RUL work also shows dependence on observation length within its simulated task \citep{AlDulaimi2019A}. SQ1 therefore requires explicit windows, ordering, normalization, pretraining and resource budgets. Selecting statistics after a bounded Transformer comparison remains a substantive answer to suitability.
-
-\subsection{Audio Representations}
-Acoustic encodings include MFCCs, log-mel spectra, wavelet maps, GASF transforms and learned features. The penetration network applies VMD and GASF, while DMAW adapts Transformer representations for welding observations \citep{Hua2026Weld,P003}. An image-shaped acoustic tensor remains sound evidence, not an independently acquired camera stream. Spectra must also use the same available interval as competing representations.
-
-The Audio Spectrogram Transformer supplies a relevant pretrained candidate, but generic audio benchmarks do not establish industrial reliability or its advantage on every fault target \citep{P011}. MIMII and ToyADMOS make background composition and noise mixing explicit \citep{Purohit2019MIMII,Koizumi2019ToyADMOS}. This supports careful recording and normalization choices rather than an automatic architecture selection. The REASSEMBLE alignment checks prevented evaluation of an acoustic predictor (Chapter~6).
-
-\subsection{Frozen Representations, Fine-Tuning, and Computational Constraints}
-
-Freezing an encoder, fitting a small head, and adapting an entire network allocate very different statistical and computational resources. Comparing them requires disclosure of which parameters change and what data influence selection. Benchmark methods based on pretrained features can be effective with nominal training data, but their representation-learning cost is partly external to the industrial experiment \citep{Roth2022Towards,Costanzino2024Multimodal}. This is legitimate transfer learning, provided that the comparison does not present it as equivalent to training from scratch.
-
-Distillation introduces another boundary between training and deployment. PGMTKD's multimodal teachers enrich a pressure-only student, while the gated acoustic--vibration study reports a distilled LightGBM student but leaves ambiguity about teacher-feature inputs and timing boundaries \citep{Li2025PGMTKD,Huang2026Rolling}. Resource claims must account for feature extraction, teachers required at inference, and preprocessing as well as the final predictor. For SQ1, the appropriate conclusion is a need to compare explicit fitting recipes under feasible budgets, not to assume that frozen features or full fine-tuning are intrinsically fairer.
-
-\section{Multimodal Fusion Strategies --- SQ2}
-
-Fusion is classified here by the location of information exchange. Early integration combines raw channels or engineered features; intermediate integration combines learned representations; output-level fusion combines predictions or anomaly scores. Hybrid systems use more than one stage. Author terminology is retained where needed, but it does not override this operational distinction.
-
-\subsection{Unimodal References and Incremental Modality Value}
-The strongest credible single-input model defines the central SQ2 reference. Welding makes the issue immediate: the penetration paper labels its own audio-only and image-only models as achieving 100\%, equal to clean fusion. Gains over other backbones cannot establish an added-modality effect \citep[PDF pp.~18, 22]{Hua2026Weld}. HANDOVER likewise reports 69.1\% for video plus force--torque and 67.9\% after adding gripper state within the I3D-D family \citep[PDF p.~7, Table~III]{Thoduka2024Handover}. These native-paper values are five-class outcome accuracies averaged over five trainings, not binary failure AP.
-
-The tool-wear comparison reports MAE 6.6183 for its proposed model, 8.3002 for visual ResNet50 and 60.5118 for its acoustic reference; other multimodal architectures have errors above 45 \citep[PDF p.~24, Table~1]{P045}. Architecture differences prevent causal attribution to fusion alone, but the strong visual reference changes the interpretation. Transferable naive multimodal failures and industrial all-channel losses reinforce the need to report incremental effects against the best admissible component \citep{Wang2020What,Goodarzi2023Deep}.
-
-\subsection{Feature Concatenation}
-Concatenation combines representations without a complex allocation mechanism, making it a necessary reference. Its performance depends on feature scale, dimension, regularization and encoder quality. M3DM's direct-concatenation ablation is below its point-cloud reference, whereas the complete hybrid system improves on both inputs \citep[Table~4]{Wang2023Multimodal}. MADFlow provides another protocol-specific alignment-versus-concatenation comparison \citep{Li2025MADFlow}.
-
-CFA supplies explicitly transferable evidence: for DLinear on Energy, first concatenation yields MSE .670 versus .263 without text and .243 with constrained fusion \citep[PDF p.~4, Table~4]{P023}. Yet its own constrained model also loses in some settings. Manufacturing fusion can similarly help one architecture and harm another under the same broad task \citep[Table~3]{Li2022A}. Concatenation should receive reasonable normalization and fitting; a weak implementation cannot establish the necessity of a more elaborate operator.
-
-\subsection{Prediction-Level and Late Fusion}
-Late fusion preserves separately fitted predictors and combines outputs through averaging, stacking or evidence rules. Thermal--vibration diagnosis uses separate CNN softmax outputs with weighted Dempster--Shafer aggregation. Its clean reported diagnostic rate is 100\%, compared with 99.06\% for IRT and 96.32\% for vibration \citep[PDF p.~7, Table~VII]{Huo2022Multisensor}. Fixed weights and the sample-CV protocol qualify the gain, but this is direct physical camera--sensor late-fusion evidence.
-
-HANDOVER compares late outputs with intermediate sensor integration into RGB and optical-flow branches \citep[PDF pp.~5--7]{Thoduka2024Handover}. Inspection map fusion is different: cross-modal mapping and CPIR aggregate discrepancy maps that need not be probabilities \citep{Costanzino2024Multimodal,Shangguan2025CPIR}. Moreover, some papers call pre-classifier representation combination late fusion \citep{Wang2020What,Xie2025Audiovisual}. A learned prediction combiner therefore requires an explicit fitting bank, calibration contract and score scale, in addition to a common outer assessment.
-
-\subsection{Gated and Context-Conditioned Fusion}
-Gates can allocate embeddings, blend maps, alter prediction coefficients or balance training objectives. DMAW reports 96.1\% against 90.5\% for summation and 92.6\% for its gate comparator \citep[PDF p.~9, Table~V]{P003}. TC-MAF instead bounds auxiliary RGB evidence around a multimodal anchor using normal-training dispersion and local disagreement \citep[PDF pp.~3--5]{P026}. These are different adaptive mechanisms.
-
-The conditioner is decisive. GCCF-AD describes dynamic fusion, but Eq.~8 specifies a learned scalar $\alpha=\sigma(\omega)$ without an explicit sample-quality input \citep[PDF p.~3]{P038}. PGMTKD's teacher allocation uses training losses, while task-loss balancing does not become a deployment quality measurement \citep{Li2025PGMTKD,P045}. GAF-Net uses progressive feature feedback, and trustworthy fusion uses evidence-based aggregation \citep{Huang2026Rolling,Ying2025Trustworthy}. These mechanisms differ in which weights can vary, which inputs determine them and whether the resulting predictions improve on a fixed combination. Process context does not itself impose physical laws.
-
-\subsection{Comparability and Fairness of Fusion Evaluations}
-A shared dataset name does not establish a matched experiment. Cohort, supervision, observation window, pretraining, feature dimensions, augmentation and selection exposure can explain gains. The tool-wear study changes architecture alongside fusion; incomplete federated imagery changes client data access as well as the integration mechanism \citep{P045,P051}. These are informative pipeline comparisons, but do not automatically isolate attention or quality conditioning.
-
-Same-evidence controls support narrower attribution. TC-MAF retains its evidence sources while comparing bounded fusion with static mean, weighted scores, rank fusion and a learned gate \citep[PDF p.~7, Table~3]{P026}. Conversely, the disagreement-aware paper improves a local released-code baseline that is below the published M3DM result \citep[PDF p.~10, Table~4]{P033}. Distilled predictors also receive training information unavailable to ordinary unimodal models \citep{Li2025PGMTKD}. Such settings are legitimate when identified. Pipeline benefit, modality necessity and a causal fusion-operator effect remain different questions.
-
-\section{Reliability and Robustness --- SQ3}
-
-Robustness is defined by an intervention and a prediction setting. Missing channels, corrupted measurements, asynchronous observations, and a new machine are different problems. The matrix records evidence for each regime separately; general robustness language is not used to fill an untested cell.
-
-\subsection{Missing Modalities}
-Planned single-input deployment differs from unannounced loss in a model expecting complete inputs. PGMTKD's pressure-only student and incomplete RGB--geometry methods establish useful reduced-input inference, while ModDrop and missing-modality Transformers provide transferable mechanisms and counterexamples \citep{Li2025PGMTKD,Sui2026Incomplete,P005,P016}. They do not establish every possible outage pattern.
-
-TC-MAF explicitly evaluates unavailable 3D: I-AUROC falls from .9788 to .9330 and P-AUPRO from .9901 to .9586 \citep[PDF p.~7, Table~3]{P026}. Federated substation work varies the fraction of incomplete clients, reaching 80\%, which differs from an arbitrary test-camera failure matrix \citep[PDF p.~29, Figure~8]{P051}. ZSG-IAD's subset-input rows do not fully specify shared-checkpoint and training exposure \citep{P025}. Missing training images in hot rolling constitute another regime \citep{Ma2025A}. Each comparison must state known masks, training availability, replacement policy and all-inputs-absent behavior.
-
-\subsection{Noise and Partial Degradation}
-Physical camera robustness studies already exist. Thermal--vibration diagnosis injects Gaussian noise and vibration bias/stuck faults; welding penetration recognition reports artificial interference; acoustic--thermal and acoustic--vibration studies examine further noise settings \citep{Huo2022Multisensor,Hua2026Weld,Ying2025Trustworthy,Huang2026Rolling}. These interventions differ in affected streams, severity and exposure.
-
-At -2~dB, noisy IRT gives 95.26\%, rising to 97.6\% with fault-free vibration; noisy vibration gives 36.86\%, rising to 94.2\% with fault-free IRT \citep[PDF p.~7, Table~VIII]{Huo2022Multisensor}. The healthy companion is part of that condition. The welding paper's interference result is less reproducible because operator, magnitude and exposure matching are unclear \citep{Hua2026Weld}. Synthetic SNR tests do not establish natural simultaneous degradation, and ordinary defective objects are targets rather than corrupted modalities. Filtering or a harsh-environment title alone cannot fill a robustness cell.
-
-\subsection{Temporal Misalignment and Distribution Shift}
-A clock offset breaks correspondence; domain shift changes the assessment distribution. Multiple sampling rates or image repetition do not demonstrate offset tolerance. Registration studies establish why alignment matters, while OmniFuser's repeated within-pass images provide a strategy rather than a synchronization-failure benchmark \citep{Petrich2021Multimodal,Chen2023Multisensor,P021}.
-
-Stronger transfer examples include training on one HANDOVER robot and assessing another, and Subway-trained rail localization evaluated on high-speed rail \citep[PDF p.~7, Table~V]{Thoduka2024Handover}\citep[PDF p.~10, Table~III]{P031}. Target-adaptive studies, target-validated condition studies and independent-build experiments have different access assumptions \citep{Wang2025A,Chu2024A,Huang2026Rolling,Bevans2023Heterogeneous}. Rebuilding normal memories on Eyecandies with fixed fusion hyperparameters is useful recipe transfer, but not untouched-target or cross-machine deployment \citep{P026,P033}. Genuine temporal offsets, calibration drift and new physical groups need separately identified protocols.
-
-\subsection{Quality-Aware and Uncertainty-Aware Fusion}
-Signal quality, confidence, calibrated probability and evidence consistency are different quantities. QMF motivates weighting through generalization upper bounds under assumptions about uncertainty, loss and a common distribution; this is not a guarantee of actual-risk superiority after arbitrary shift \citep[PDF pp.~4--5]{Zhang2023QMF}. The transferable UDML experiments train on controlled Gaussian intensities and acknowledge that sample difficulty can be mistaken for modality corruption \citep[PDF pp.~4--5, 9]{P022}.
-
-QMF itself reports NYU scene-classification accuracy of 70.09\% on clean inputs, below concatenation at 70.30\%, but 55.60\% at corruption intensity ten, above late fusion at 51.99\% \citep[PDF p.~8, Table~1]{Zhang2023QMF}. These non-industrial RGB--depth results demonstrate a regime-specific benefit rather than universal dominance.
-
-Results can also conflict across endpoints. The disagreement add-on improves local MVTec image AUROC from .779 to .800 but reduces Eyecandies image AUROC from .810 to .794 while improving pixel metrics \citep[PDF pp.~10--11, Tables~4--5]{P033}. TC-MAF's training-dispersion proxy has a smaller contribution than its evidence/fusion structure \citep{P026}. These findings require validation of the conditioning signal and outcomes; changing attention, an uncertainty plot or an evidence mass does not by itself establish calibrated reliability.
-
-\subsection{Modality Dropout, Augmentation, and Fallback Mechanisms}
-Training dropout exposes a model to selected absence patterns, imputation generates substitutes and deterministic fallback routes to an available predictor. These mechanisms can yield the same output under some conditions without learning the same recovery. Survivor routing should therefore be evaluated as a policy, while corrupted-but-present inputs test a different prediction problem.
-
-Fuzzy regularization represents failures through partial corruption and whole-input replacement by constants or noise, evaluating Gazebo pose regression under random sample splits of overlapping windows \citep[PDF pp.~3--5]{Altinses2023Fuzzy}. This does not establish robustness to an unavailable input branch in a physical deployment. OmniFuser asserts dropout and masking without a quantified outage table \citep[PDF p.~10]{P021}. Generated replacements can harm: replacing clean depth with CycleGAN output worsens a synthetic robot benchmark's MSE from $2.1745\times10^{-3}$ to $13.472\times10^{-3}$ \citep[Table~7]{Altinses2025Performance}. Exposure-matched static controls, explicit replacements and surviving-input references are consequently necessary to distinguish learned recovery from imposed logic.
-
-\section{Evaluation Beyond Accuracy --- SQ4}
-
-Practical usefulness depends on how predictions are assessed, selected, and interpreted. Accuracy can be high when the target prevalence, grouping, or threshold makes an experiment easier than its intended deployment. SQ4 therefore requires a broader account of evidence than a single aggregate score.
-
-\subsection{Grouped Evaluation and Leakage Prevention}
-The independent unit may be a weld, tool, participant, recording, machine or build. Random windows can share acquisition context or raw samples. Fuzzy regularization forms twenty-step windows with stride one and randomly partitions examples; unseen-trajectory assessment is not established \citep[PDF pp.~4--5]{Altinses2023Fuzzy}. Weld-run grouping is also unresolved in the closest welding studies \citep{P003,Hua2026Weld}. These observations identify dependence risks without measuring an invented leakage magnitude.
-
-HANDOVER supplies an explicit participant-disjoint official split, while an adapted implementation still needs participant information for safe inner folds \citep{Thoduka2024Handover}. Normalization, feature selection and HPO need the same separation \citep{P004,P017}. Out-of-fold stacking can protect outer assessment, but that does not prove every deeper meta-validation dependency is absent. Repeatedly choosing follow-ups after observing assessment outcomes also creates selection exposure even when each fit excludes its outer test labels.
-
-\subsection{Discrimination, Calibration, and Decision Thresholds}
-Ranking, probability quality and thresholded decisions answer different questions. AUROC does not specify a false-alarm operating point or calibrated probability. MVTec AD shows that good ranking can coexist with poor thresholded localization, while strict-FPR metrics reward another aspect of detection \citep{Bergmann2019MVTec,Costanzino2024Multimodal,Cheng2025Multimodal}.
-
-The rail vision-language paper reports pixel metrics and optimal-threshold F1; these are not image-level alarm accuracy under a training-selected threshold \citep[PDF p.~7]{P031}. In manufacturing, accuracy .9921 alongside precision .2487 on an imbalanced layer illustrates the effect of prevalence and threshold \citep{Petrich2021Multimodal}. Validation-fitted calibration can improve in-distribution probability quality, while dataset shift can undermine confidence estimates \citep{Guo2017Calibration,P008}. Proper losses such as NLL and Brier assess probability forecasts, with an explicit distinction between loss orientation, calibration and discrimination \citep{P055}. A generated confidence field or evidence mass cannot replace probability-quality evaluation. Threshold selection belongs to admissible training/validation, while assessment reveals the resulting trade-off.
-
-\subsection{Uncertainty and Statistical Comparisons}
-Seed variation, acquisition-group sampling and selection uncertainty are distinct. HANDOVER's standard deviations describe five trainings on a fixed test set, whereas a group bootstrap of saved predictions samples assessment units conditional on fitted models \citep{Thoduka2024Handover}. Neither automatically includes all retraining or domain uncertainty.
-
-CV folds share training data. The classical variance result establishes that no universally unbiased CV-variance estimator exists under its stated assumptions; ignoring covariance can substantially underestimate uncertainty \citep[PDF pp.~8--10]{Bengio2004CVVariance}. This does not prohibit useful conditional intervals. It requires an explicit estimand and dependence model. Small fusion effects, unclear repetition counts and contradictory significance prose need similar caution \citep{Gu2024Improved,Xu2025ModalityResilient}. Paired effects on common cases are preferable to unrelated score intervals. A zero-containing interval does not establish equivalence, and a positive point estimate does not establish practical importance without a meaningful criterion.
-
-\subsection{Interpretability and Its Limits}
-Scores, coefficients, gate traces and maps can expose model behavior without supplying a causal explanation. The attention debate includes both counterexamples to faithfulness and evidence that trained weights contain useful information; the appropriate conclusion is task-specific evaluation, rather than a title-level rule \citep{P036}. Allocation changes alone do not show why a model predicts correctly.
-
-ZSG-IAD measures schema validity, language consistency and coarse-to-final-mask overlap. These support particular forms of self-consistency, not independent physical defect causation or calibrated confidence \citep{P025}. HANDOVER similarly leaves explanation through human-action predictions as future research \citep{Thoduka2024Handover}. In the thesis, observable quality responses and inspectable coefficients can support descriptive transparency. Expert understanding, causal attribution and usability require further evidence. Process context must also be distinguished from a physical equation constraint such as PGMTKD's pressure model \citep{Li2025PGMTKD}.
-
-\subsection{Modularity, Reproducibility, and Computational Cost}
-A modular pipeline enables controlled replacement, but does not itself establish robustness. An identified version, accessible code, executable processing and independently reproduced findings provide different assurances \citep{P009}. The local-versus-published M3DM discrepancy illustrates why dependencies, valid-point filtering, checkpoints and scoring conventions matter \citep{P033}.
-
-Cost comparisons also need a common boundary. CPIR reports lower FPS than CFM, exposing a performance--speed trade-off \citep{Shangguan2025CPIR}. A lightweight image network's 5.65~ms excludes an independent sensor branch and acquisition delay \citep[PDF p.~10, Table~V]{P035}. TimeSformer's cost depends on ImageNet pretraining and workload \citep{P034}. Hardware, batch size, caching, synchronization and included stages determine what can be compared. Prospective deployment and operator usability remain separate validation questions rather than consequences of a successful offline forward pass.
-
-\section{Critical Synthesis and Implications for This Thesis}
-The strongest precedents for the welding question combine observations of the same physical operation and evaluate them against a defined outcome. RGB--geometry inspection, robot simulation, and studies of learning with missing inputs contribute evidence about particular components. Their results have different implications for the five implemented studies because the target, observation window, and conditions of deployment change.
-
-\subsection{Findings Supported Across the Reviewed Studies}
-Distinct physical observations can improve a task-specific pipeline. DMAW, thermal--vibration diagnosis, HANDOVER and tool-wear prediction provide relevant examples under their respective protocols \citep{P003,Huo2022Multisensor,Thoduka2024Handover,P045}. Controlled evidence combination can also improve RGB--geometry inspection, including a bounded rule with an explicit missing-3D result \citep{P026}. A broad claim that these combinations or reliability approaches are absent is therefore untenable.
-
-The evidence does not select a universal winner. Planned single-input deployment, complete absence, synthetic degradation and target-domain adaptation remain different achievements \citep{Li2025PGMTKD,Altinses2023Fuzzy,P051}. Strong representations, labels and observation windows can matter as much as integration. The supported finding is conditional benefit under a defined task and protocol, with distinct limitations in acquisition independence, uncertainty and operational validation.
-
-\subsection{Conflicting Findings and Unresolved Questions}
-Negative findings are substantive evidence. Welding unimodal equality, lower HANDOVER performance after adding gripper state, worse multimodal tool-wear alternatives than a strong visual reference and Eyecandies image-score deterioration challenge unconditional fusion benefit \citep{Hua2026Weld,Thoduka2024Handover,P045,P033}. Category-specific geometry losses, harmful hydraulic-channel combinations and naive multimodal optimization failures reinforce that conclusion \citep{Horwitz2023Back,Bonfiglioli2023The,Goodarzi2023Deep,Wang2020What}.
-
-Possible explanations include redundancy, optimization, noise, supervision and limited data; these studies do not identify one universal cause. Questions about independent groups, matched static controls, informative quality conditioning and calibrated risk remain protocol-specific. Where tables within a study disagree, the magnitude of the reported effect remains uncertain.
-
-\subsection{Implications for Representation and Fusion Selection}
-The welding results suggest an order for comparing architectures. First establish how well each physical input predicts the chosen target under a feasible fitting procedure (SQ1). Then compare equal averaging, a fitted static combination, and a context-dependent alternative with the strongest constituent predictor on the same assessment cases (SQ2). If the learned combination introduces another calibration step, a gain over a single branch estimates the benefit of the fitted pipeline. Isolating the contribution of the second input also requires a single-input control with the same opportunity for recalibration. If a gate is intended to handle failures, its static comparator needs comparable exposure to those failures during training (SQ3). The uncertainty of each contrast depends on the physical units that were independently assessed (SQ4).
-
-\subsection{Implications for Experimental Design}
-\label{sec:literature-study-gaps}
-The following comparisons link the five implementations to the main question. Each starts from the closest published evidence and identifies what remains uncertain for the target actually available in that study.
-
-\paragraph{PHM gear monitoring (SQ1, SQ2, and SQ4).}
-Post-build computed tomography can provide an independent reference for locating flaws in manufacturing \citep{Petrich2021Multimodal}. In tool-wear prediction, an image taken after a pass and acoustic emission recorded during cutting have also been used together to estimate wear \citep[PDF pp.~19--24]{P045}. PHM poses a different measurement problem. Its gear photographs were collected periodically, while the challenge specifies sensor-only operation at deployment \citep{PHM2026Challenge}. The unresolved question is whether available sensor groups can predict a continuous condition proxy derived from post-run photographs across held-out gear experiments. Chapter~5 compares sensor representations and fusion of their embeddings with a training-mean reference (SQ1 and SQ2). Error in percentage points describes prediction of the photographic proxy (SQ4). Establishing its relation to physical tooth damage would require an independent measurement. Inference-time loss of a camera falls outside that comparison.
-
-\paragraph{REASSEMBLE robotic assembly (SQ1--SQ4).}
-The REASSEMBLE dataset paper benchmarks action segmentation and visual condition checks \citep[PDF pp.~10--13]{Sliwowski2025REASSEMBLE}. Published handover experiments already demonstrate multimodal classification of completed robotic trials \citep[Table~III]{Thoduka2024Handover}. For failed assembly actions, two comparisons remain open. Can a fitted video--sensor model rank failures better than a strong statistical sensor model? Does process-dependent weighting improve on equal averaging? Chapter~6 holds out complete recordings and compares visual, statistical-sensor, and time-series representations (SQ1), followed by feature concatenation, averaging, stacking, and gating on the same eligible actions (SQ2). Known absence and controlled corruption address different reasons to distrust an input (SQ3). Paired recording-level uncertainty, probability errors, and decisions at selected thresholds qualify the performance differences (SQ4). The observation window extends through action completion.
-
-\paragraph{MSM milling (SQ1--SQ4).}
-OmniFuser forecasts milling-tool wear states and cutting force \citep[PDF p.~11, Tables~IV--V]{P021}. A visual--acoustic study estimates tool wear after a cutting pass \citep[PDF p.~24, Table~1]{P045}. The MSM dataset instead identifies abnormal cutting intervals that warrant intervention. Its descriptor distinguishes these intervals from stable cutting with a defective tool and documents camera coverage in the laboratory \citep{Kim2026MSM}. The open comparison is whether video improves on machine telemetry for that interval-level distinction within cuts observed by both streams. Chapter~\ref{ch:msm} tests representations and static fusion against their own single-input predictors (SQ1 and SQ2), then compares performance under missing or degraded inputs (SQ3). Grouped evaluation and the potential disagreement between ranking and probability scores address SQ4. The covered laboratory dates bound the claim about new operating conditions.
-
-\paragraph{ViFailback manipulation (SQ1, SQ2, and SQ4).}
-\citet[PDF pp.~7--8, Tables~2--4]{Zeng2026ViFailback} use visual symbols to diagnose failures, evaluate responses to questions, and guide corrective actions. For the binary outcome of a complete manipulation episode on a held-out task, two comparisons remain open. Does adding a generic visual representation improve on robot-state measurements, and does transferring a visual encoder from REASSEMBLE change that comparison? Chapter~\ref{ch:vifailback} compares generic frozen features with those of the transferred encoder (SQ1) and assesses probability fusion against the constituent sensor model (SQ2). Separately reporting success and failure ranking and their macro-average makes the minority outcome visible (SQ4). The defined corruption procedures lacked a completed stress evaluation for the compared models, leaving the reliability question (SQ3) open for this task.
-
-\paragraph{HANDOVER human--robot interaction (SQ1--SQ4).}
-The published HANDOVER benchmark compares unimodal and fused models for five outcome classes. Within its I3D comparison, video plus force--torque signals achieved higher mean accuracy than the combination that also included gripper state \citep[PDF p.~7, Table~III]{Thoduka2024Handover}. The supplementary thesis study asks a narrower question. Over a common observation window, does averaging video and wrench predictions improve the ranking of binary handover failures over the better single-input model? Appendix~\ref{app:handover} compares the two representations and fixed averaging (SQ1 and SQ2), examines sensor noise, blur, and fallback to one input (SQ3), and assesses decisions for each outcome class (SQ4). The published participant-disjoint split supports the official test comparison. The missing participant-to-trial mapping prevented a separate participant-disjoint fit of the planned learned stacker.
-
-\medskip
-\noindent The five studies thus test different parts of the method proposed for welding. Their results can establish when a representation, fusion rule, or fallback policy helps on its own target. A welding-specific answer still requires an independently assessed weld-quality outcome, a defined decision time, and paired observations on welding sessions reserved for that assessment. Chapter~\ref{ch:discussion} interprets these comparisons.
+\chapter{Critical Literature Review}
+
+\label{ch:literature}
+
+\section*{Multimodal Learning in Industrial and Robotic Monitoring}
+
+\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Multimodal Learning in Industrial and Robotic Monitoring}
+
+Industrial monitoring covers tasks such as fault detection, checking a system's physical condition,
+assessing manufacturing quality, and confirming that a robot has completed its task. The traditional
+approach uses single sensor readings and engineered features to characterise the system's behaviour.
+Learned representations take this further by utilising data from images, sound and sensor sequences.
+Multimodal learning examines how information from different modalities can be combined to support a
+common prediction task \parencite{Baltrusaitis2019Multimodal,Huo2022Multisensor,Thoduka2024Handover}.
+
+The literature touches on several related areas of research: traditional feature engineering, the
+use of pre-trained representations, modelling over time, feature combination, prediction fusion, and
+adaptive methods (where the method can adapt to the context). They differ in the information they
+require, their computational cost, the level of supervision they need, and their sensitivity to
+input quality.
+
+Industrial and robotic monitoring provides a platform on which to compare these approaches as the
+process can be monitored via a number of different modalities (cameras, acoustic sensors, machine
+sensors, etc.). Cameras will obviously provide a view of the visible process, whereas the acoustic
+and machine sensors provide information which is not visible. The benefit of combining data depends
+heavily on what each modality retains, the specific prediction you're trying to make, and the
+system's test conditions \parencite{Wang2020What,Hua2026Weld,Goodarzi2023Deep}. Simply increasing the
+amount of data does not necessarily lead to better predictions.
+
+Robotic welding is the principal driver of this thesis. Work on welding using visual and acoustic
+data has shown that multimodal integration can improve prediction performance, but also that, in
+some cases, adding a further modality provides no benefit at all \parencite{P003,Hua2026Weld}. The Intel
+Welding audit documented prior examination of the proposed assessment population, so an independent
+assessment of weld quality was not possible on that population (see Chapter~\ref{ch:introduction}).
+The review therefore addresses a more general architectural question across a range of industrial
+and robotic monitoring tasks: how physical measurements, visual data, and sensor data can be
+combined to assess system condition and task outcome.
+
+This chapter is organised around four research sub-questions: (SQ1) the appropriateness of the
+representations, (SQ2) the incremental value of data integration, (SQ3) reliability in the face of
+missing or degraded inputs and (SQ4) the need for evaluation criteria that go beyond prediction
+accuracy. The aim is to present the scientific evidence, identify the gaps in the comparisons and
+describe the experimental design requirements. The architecture is detailed in
+Chapter~\ref{ch:foundations} and the experimental methodology in Chapter~\ref{ch:methodology}.
+
+\section{Review Scope and Search Methodology}
+
+The review examines industrial fusion claims through the methodologies, comparison tables, and
+experimental results of the papers. It focuses on the link between the proposed architecture, the
+available physical observations, the prediction target, and the assessment protocol.
+
+A research gap is defined as a comparison that the existing body of scientific evidence does not
+address for a given target, input set and assessment population. The ability to identify such a gap
+will, of course, depend on the search strategy and the amount of published literature available.
+
+\subsection{Research Themes and Search Questions}
+
+The representation theme concerns what is being observed, what transformations are being applied,
+and whether the representation retains the information relevant to the task at hand. The fusion
+theme is about whether adding further inputs improves performance on a standard prediction task
+compared with a set of plausible single-input references.
+
+The robustness theme looks at which absence, corruption, and distribution-change regimes were
+assessed. The evaluation theme looks at assessment independence, thresholds, uncertainty, resource
+measurement and reproducibility.
+
+These themes intentionally include negative and inconclusive findings. Comparisons where an
+additional modality yields minimal or no benefit can provide valuable insights into representation
+suitability, redundancy, and the limitations of the evaluated fusion procedure.
+
+The review clearly distinguishes between two objectives that are often confused. Training with
+multiple modalities to deploy a single-input model is a privileged training scenario. Combining
+multiple available inputs at deployment is a multimodal inference problem. Cross-modal distillation
+and reconstruction link the two objectives but the acquisition assumptions are very different.
+
+The taxonomy of \textcite{Baltrusaitis2019Multimodal} is the organising terminology. The industrial claims are then assessed against the original
+studies rather than being entirely inferred from that survey.
+
+\subsection{Sources, Search Terms, and Search Period}
+
+The literature review was carried out as part of the project. The initial search was focused on the
+period 2016 to 2026 with a selection of earlier methodological papers also included. Of the 120
+papers identified, 39 met the inclusion criteria and 37 were fully appraised.
+
+A subsequent expansion identified 57 distinct full-text files after duplicate removal and version
+verification. The comparison register comprises 94 unique work records, representing a curated
+selection rather than an estimate of the entire research field.
+
+Searches were conducted pairing industrial monitoring and manufacturing terms with fusion, stage,
+attention, missing inputs, noise and domain shift. Dataset searches included MVTec AD, MVTec 3D-AD,
+Eyecandies, bearing datasets and machine sound benchmarks.
+
+Publisher and repository searches facilitated publication retrieval. Crossref supplied metadata for
+identity verification \parencite{Crossref2020REST}, while OpenAlex supported citation tracing
+\parencite{Priem2022OpenAlex}. Zotero was employed to link bibliographic records with PDFs and appraisal
+notes \parencite{Zotero2026}.
+
+Appendix~\ref{app:literature} differentiates the initial screening population from the subsequent
+full-text appraisal. The seminal stacked generalisation paper by \textcite{Ting1999Issues} was included
+to support the discussion of prediction-level fusion methodology,
+representing a separate addition to the historical full-text appraisal count.
+
+\subsection{Inclusion and Exclusion Criteria}
+
+Direct industrial work focuses on faults, physical condition, manufacturing quality, inspection, and
+the outcome of a robotic task. Dataset papers and legitimate unimodal methods are also relevant, as
+fusion depends on a well-defined prediction target and a suitable reference.
+
+Transferable work is explicitly identified. Work on multimodal optimisation failure modes,
+missing-input mechanisms, uncertainty weighting, and evaluation theory can all be valuable in
+designing industrial architectures, even if they do not amount to industrial validation
+\parencite{Wang2020What,P016,P023,Bengio2004CVVariance}.
+
+A publication may be included in the register as background or as a methodological contribution.
+Simulated robot regression, RGB-geometry inspection, physical camera-telemetry monitoring and
+single-camera feature-level fusion are different experimental settings
+\parencite{Altinses2023Fuzzy,P026,Thoduka2024Handover,P035}.
+
+Inaccessible evidence is not taken to be irrelevant, and missing reporting does not establish that a
+particular operation was not performed. The language used by the individual authors is interpreted
+in the context of the actual acquisition and evaluation procedure to determine whether the study
+yields multimodal observations, inference-time robustness evidence, or an equivalent unimodal
+reference.
+
+\subsection{Evidence Extraction and Critical Appraisal}
+
+Each appraisal records the source identity and version inspected, the prediction target and unit,
+the physical inputs, the transformations, the fusion method, the comparison models, the
+partitioning, the training access, and the reliability interventions.
+
+Experimental results are linked to pages, tables and figures. Where possible, decisive PDF tables
+have been checked, as text extraction may obscure input columns, numerical values, or footnotes.
+
+The appraisal records both controlled and varying factors. A common assessment cohort supports
+comparisons between fitted pipelines. To isolate fusion's contribution, however, differences in
+encoders, supervision, augmentation, and model capacity must also be considered.
+
+Input subset models, complete inference-time absence, corrupted present data and target domain
+adaptation are all recorded separately.
+
+The comparison matrix in Appendix~\ref{app:literature} contains the work-level evidence. The compact
+classification codes are interpreted alongside the relevant protocol descriptions and source
+locators.
+
+\subsection{Limitations of the Review}
+
+The papers reviewed are by no means exhaustive and are not statistically independent. Several
+inspection studies use the same MVTec 3D-AD or Eyecandies dataset, so improvements on those
+benchmarks are not necessarily indicative of improvements on independent industrial installations.
+The preprints are also version-specific, and some publication details are still provisional.
+
+When relatively few studies examine a particular condition, the body of evidence is such that any
+conclusions about that condition are necessarily weak.
+
+The main MVTec 3D-AD table gives 249 normal and 948 anomalous test samples (total 1197)
+\parencite[Table~1]{Bergmann2022The}. The welding penetration paper reports inconsistent acquisition and
+training numbers, and the light inspection paper reports different NwfDD values in its main table
+and its ablation table \parencite[pp.~6708, 6711]{Hua2026Weld}; \parencite[p.~6661, Tables~IV, VI--VII]{P035}.
+
+These inconsistencies reduce the precision of the comparisons. The studies remain useful when their
+methods and reported findings are interpreted within clearly stated limits.
+
+\section{Industrial Monitoring Tasks and Supervision}
+
+Different monitoring tasks rely on different assumptions about training data, available
+observations, and acceptable levels of prediction error. These assumptions must be understood prior
+to comparing representation learning and fusion architectures.
+
+The definition of the prediction target is absolutely crucial, as performance on one industrial task
+cannot be considered valid evidence of performance on another.
+
+\subsection{Anomaly Detection, Defect Classification, and Failure Classification}
+
+Normal-only anomaly detection learns a reference for nominal observations. Supervised defect
+classification, by contrast, uses labelled fault or quality classes. MVTec AD and MVTec 3D-AD are
+examples of the normal-only approach, whereas welding penetration recognition is an example of
+supervised classification \parencite{Bergmann2019MVTec,Bergmann2022The,Hua2026Weld}.
+
+HANDOVER defines five completed-interaction outcome classes: success, no approach, no grasp, no
+release and object drop \parencite{Thoduka2024Handover}. A task failure in the operational domain
+doesn't necessarily mean that some physical damage has occurred or that we can identify the root
+cause of that damage.
+
+Supervision is thus an important aspect of the evaluation. The weakly supervised inspection study
+brings the MVTec data back in, as training requires image-level anomalous labels, which of course
+changes the normal-only setting \parencite[6659]{P035}.
+
+ZSG-IAD trains on reports and masks before evaluating on held-out categories or datasets. The
+zero-shot terminology refers to that assessment boundary rather than to the complete absence of
+anomaly supervised training \parencite[PDF pp.~6, 8--9]{P025}.
+
+These differences establish the need to compare architectures against their respective target
+definitions and under their respective supervision conditions.
+
+\subsection{Condition Estimation and Regression}
+
+Continuous prediction calls for attention to measurement units, reference observations, and the
+horizon at which the target is defined. Remaining useful life is a very different prediction problem
+from instantaneous defect probability.
+
+\textcite{AlDulaimi2019A} look at a hybrid CNN-LSTM on simulated C-MAPSS trajectories using RMSE and an
+asymmetric prognostic score. Both branches of their model process the same
+set of selected telemetry channels. The study therefore provides evidence of complementary
+representations of the sensor data rather than any physical camera/sensor complementarity.
+
+The synthetic robot benchmarks of \textcite{Altinses2025Performance} examine cross-modal prediction,
+reconstruction, and downstream regression. They show the importance
+of relationships between representations, though errors on different reconstructed outputs do not
+necessarily tell you which modality is more useful for a given common prediction task.
+
+This issue is particularly relevant to proxy supervision. A model may accurately predict a derived
+condition index, yet the relationship between that index and actual physical deterioration may
+remain uncertain. A defensible regression comparison must therefore define the target construction,
+measurement scale, fitted pre-processing, and common assessment observations before interpreting
+numerical improvement as indicative of enhanced condition monitoring.
+
+\subsection{Dataset-Provided Labels, Derived Targets, and Proxy Supervision}
+
+Reference labels have very different evidential roles. Post-build CT provides flaw localisation for
+the voxel-level monitoring study by \textcite{Petrich2021Multimodal}. Microscopy and
+density assessments are used to define quality classes in \textcite{Li2022A}. Operando X-ray
+imaging is used to define process-state labels in \textcite{Pandiyan2022Deep}.
+
+These studies show how an independent instrument can establish or validate a prediction target
+without necessarily being available at the time of model inference. It is vital to distinguish
+between supervision and predictive inputs when describing the information available to a monitoring
+architecture.
+
+For derived labels, predictive performance must be distinguished from independent physical
+validation. If the target is computed from the same measurements supplied to the model, then a low
+prediction error may reflect the model recovering that calculation rather than identifying an
+independent physical state.
+
+At the regional level, a single quality label may be assigned to many adjacent observations. Their
+spatial or temporal dependence affects the choice of an appropriate assessment unit.
+
+The empirical work in this thesis therefore distinguishes between native dataset annotations,
+independent reference measurements, and analytical proxies. Each target type yields different
+conclusions regarding physical state and operational outcome.
+
+\subsection{Retrospective Assessment and Online Monitoring}
+
+The observation horizon is what determines when the model's forecast becomes available. A predictor
+assessed after the full observation period has elapsed is effectively given a retrospective
+assessment unless its inputs are restricted to an earlier decision time.
+
+HANDOVER is very clear that its baselines classify outcomes post-trial, with causal online
+prediction still being work in progress \parencite[PDF p.~7 Section~VI]{Thoduka2024Handover}. A
+post-pass photo, a layer summary and the completed action all provide different information horizons
+from prospective monitoring \parencite{P045,Petrich2021Multimodal}.
+
+Retrospective classification is still useful for assessing representations and fusion techniques,
+though its use in an operational context differs from that of an early warning system.
+
+Computational delay must also be distinguished from observation time. DMAW uses 20 frames at 37~fps
+and reports a much shorter forward-pass latency. As a result, approximately half a second of
+observation is distinct from computational delay \parencite[PDF pp.~6--7]{P003}.
+
+Similarly, simply varying the input duration of a CNN--LSTM does not, by itself, mean that a warning
+can be generated before a defect occurs \parencite{Pandiyan2022Deep}. Active monitoring requires a
+defined information cut-off, acquisition delay and end-to-end prediction workload.
+
+\section{Multimodal Data in Industrial Applications}
+
+Complementarity is determined by the additional task-relevant information provided by an observation
+or representation, rather than by differences in tensor shape or the number of acquisition sources.
+
+Different representations of the same physical quantity can contain complementary information.
+Likewise, physically distinct sensors can provide redundant or only very weakly relevant information
+for a given prediction target.
+
+The review therefore distinguishes between physical acquisition channels and derived representations
+and considers ordinary pairing separately from cases requiring stronger correspondence, such as
+cross-modal reconstruction or monitoring of a localised process.
+
+\subsection{Visual and Video Observations}
+
+RGB images, thermal images, process videos and geometric observations all have different acquisition
+assumptions. Depth, depth-derived point clouds, and estimated surface normals can all be multiple
+views of the same geometric source.
+
+GPAD combines RGB-depth features with point cloud priors \parencite[PDF pp.~4--7]{P024}. This is an
+example of integration across representations that do not necessarily require three separate
+physical acquisition sources.
+
+RGB-geometry benchmarks describe appearance and shape rather than controller dynamics or motor
+current \parencite{Bergmann2022The,Bonfiglioli2023The}.
+
+Physical camera-plus-measurement precedents include thermal imaging with vibration, welding images
+with sound, tool images with acoustic emission, and robot video with force/torque and gripper state
+\parencite{Huo2022Multisensor,Hua2026Weld,P045,Thoduka2024Handover}.
+
+Their prediction units range from static inspection through to process windows and full trials. In
+the tool wear study, the camera is used post-cutting pass, with the preceding acoustic emission
+capturing the active cutting \parencite[PDF pp.~19--22]{P045}.
+
+The representation of visual observations also influences the information available. Both pooled
+frame features and video Transformers process multiple images, but they differ in how they treat
+temporal relationships.
+
+These distinctions between them lead us to explicitly consider visual acquisition, observation
+windows, and aggregation within multimodal monitoring architectures.
+
+\subsection{Sensor Time Series and Process Measurements}
+
+Machine measurements can vary enormously in their physical origin and in their semantic meaning. An
+accelerometer measures vibration, a pressure sensor measures fluid behaviour, and controller logs
+can contain commands, estimates, or tracking errors rather than a direct measure of a physical
+quantity.
+
+The harmonic-drive study combines high-frequency vibration with servo information, whereas PGMTKD
+transfers pressure-vibration teacher knowledge to a pressure-only student
+\parencite{Yang2024Fault,Li2025PGMTKD}.
+
+Both papers address incomplete information, but their sensor availability and deployment reasons
+differ.
+
+Representations can also inflate the apparent number of modalities without including any new
+measurements. \textcite{Kullu2022A} combine current and vibration using both raw and STFT representations.
+\textcite{Wan2024Pipeline} derive statistical 'vibration' features from two acoustic sensors
+without acquiring an accelerometer channel at all. \textcite{Wang2025A} combine
+source domain features with the decisions from the vibration data.
+
+These studies are useful as architectural comparators, as they examine different information
+representations. Their results should, of course, be treated as distinct from comparisons that
+incorporate additional physical acquisition sources.
+
+An acquisition inventory and a description of the retained representations are required prior to
+attributing any predictive gain to modality diversity.
+
+\subsection{Audio Observations}
+
+Audio can pick up machine/process events that are only very weakly visible. The relationship to
+fault state depends heavily on background conditions, mic location, and sync.
+
+MIMII deliberately incorporates factory background recordings at a range of signal-to-noise ratios.
+Its fan baseline AUC drops from .94 at $+6$~dB to .70 at $-6$~dB \parencite[Table~3]{Purohit2019MIMII}.
+
+ToyADMOS provides miniature machine recordings and an explicit noise-mixing protocol, making it a
+useful reference for acoustic monitoring \parencite{Koizumi2019ToyADMOS}.
+
+In process monitoring, airborne acoustic signals can be paired with either a melt pool camera or a
+photodiode. Signal-to-image encoding in the study by \textcite{Li2022A} is an acoustic-optical-signal experiment,
+and \textcite{Xie2025Audiovisual} use actual camera images.
+
+DCASE2020 Task2 provides audio-only baselines under an evaluation protocol that differs from that of
+later domain shift challenges \parencite{Koizumi2020Description}.
+
+Together, these studies motivate acoustic representations as candidates for industrial multimodal
+monitoring. Their utility will depend on the availability of appropriate recordings, well-defined
+targets, and correspondence with other observations. The thesis retains this scientific motivation
+even in the absence of a fully realised predictive audio comparison in the empirical work.
+
+\subsection{Synchronisation, Correspondence, and Modality Availability}
+
+Fusion requires observations that refer to a compatible event, region or time interval. Predicting
+one modality from another or combining aligned spatial discrepancy maps requires stronger
+correspondence.
+
+RGB-point cloud feature mapping uses registered benchmark geometry. Process monitoring may instead
+require time synchronisation and projection onto a common spatial grid
+\parencite{Costanzino2024Multimodal,Petrich2021Multimodal}.
+
+The digital twin study maps sensor features to robot TCP positions, showing how position can
+establish correspondence even if it is not, in any normal sense, a predictive feature
+\parencite{Chen2023Multisensor}.
+
+Modality availability has several levels. A file can exist without having a usable paired
+observation for a given prediction unit. A file can have a successfully paired observation which is
+still degraded.
+
+The acoustic-photodiode study explicitly lacks modality registration, which limits claims about
+correspondence between its two encoded streams \parencite{Li2022A}.
+
+For camera and machine sensor monitoring, we can have an explicit pairing rule that picks up
+unmatched events even if both underlying files are present. Modality completeness should therefore
+be assessed on a prediction-unit basis rather than inferred from the presence or absence of the
+files.
+
+\section{Representation Learning for Industrial Monitoring --- SQ1}
+
+SQ1 concerns the relationship between representations and the requirements of an industrial
+prediction task.
+
+The literature reviewed supports both engineered and learned representations. Which is more
+appropriate will depend on the level of supervision, the level of preprocessing, the level of
+independent sample support, the information required, and the resources available for model fitting.
+
+This section sets out the scientific justification for considering these alternatives. The details
+of their architecture and implementation are given in Chapter~\ref{ch:foundations}.
+
+\subsection{Engineered Features and Conventional Machine Learning}
+
+Engineered summaries impose a constraint on the information that a predictor can use. Measures of
+location, spread, amplitude, and trend can describe an operating state without requiring a
+high-capacity sequence model to be estimated from a small number of independent observations.
+
+The literature is replete with evidence of compact representations. Hand-crafted local geometry is
+competitive in 3D inspection, and a hydraulic CNN paper reports 1.7\% error for its best single
+sensor versus 20.5\% error for all seventeen sensors \parencite{Horwitz2023Back,Goodarzi2023Deep}.
+
+These results highlight the necessity for robust conventional references and careful selection of
+predictive information. The appropriateness of the specific 220-feature REASSEMBLE representation is
+established through task-specific comparisons in Chapter 6, rather than assumed based on prior work.
+
+Industrial vision also offers lightweight alternatives. The weakly supervised inspection network
+uses a single camera, internal anomaly-aware features, and a compact CNN. Reported computational
+cost is 2.33 million parameters and 5.65~ms per image \parencite[p.~6661, Table~V]{P035}.
+
+The study provides some relevant evidence on representation efficiency, but the anomalous training
+labels and modified MVTec supervision differ significantly from normal-only inspection.
+
+Conventional and learned representations should be compared using the same prediction target,
+assessment observations, and appropriately fitted preprocessing, rather than ranked solely by model
+family.
+
+\subsection{Pretrained Visual Representations}
+
+Pretrained visual representations provide a strong scientific explanation for transferring knowledge
+learned from large image collections to industrial applications with far less supervision. The
+features they learn may well contain the local appearance, texture, and object configuration
+relevant to the downstream prediction task.
+
+PatchCore successfully employs pre-trained, locally aggregated intermediate CNN features together
+with a memory of nominal patches for industrial inspection \parencite[Section~3]{Roth2022Towards}.
+
+This provides evidence for investigating transferred spatial representations, though its
+nominal-only patch retrieval differs significantly from supervised action classification.
+
+RT-DETR provides another architectural precedent in the form of its multi-scale visual backbone
+\parencite[Sections~4.1--4.2]{Zhao2024RTDETR}. Features at several spatial scales would be viable
+candidates for the prediction target.
+
+Its published detection accuracy and computational performance pertain to the complete detection
+architecture rather than the downstream classification performance of the extracted CNN features.
+Chapter~\ref{ch:foundations} therefore distinguishes between the components of the first-generation
+detector and those used in the thesis implementation.
+
+Asymmetric student-teacher and cross-modal mapping methods also provide further evidence for
+task-specific use of the learned visual representations
+\parencite{Rudolph2023Asymmetric,Costanzino2024Multimodal}.
+
+Vision-language inspection introduces semantic information as another potential source of
+representation value. DSE-AD combines historical and current rail images with normal and abnormal
+position prompts and assesses pixel localisation on a further rail domain \parencite{P031}.
+
+Its pair-reference and prompt assumptions differ significantly from those of isolated image
+classification.
+
+Together, these studies point to pretrained visual features as plausible candidates, while also
+highlighting the need to assess their actual information content, trainable parameters, and
+observation horizon in the downstream monitoring task.
+
+\subsection{Temporal and Time-Series Representations}
+
+Temporal representation learning provides an opportunity to retain sequence information that would
+otherwise be lost in engineered summaries.
+
+PatchTST addresses this through patch-based time-series representations and shared modelling across
+channels \parencite[Sections~3.1--3.2]{Nie2023PatchTST}.
+
+On the Weather dataset at a forecasting horizon of 96, the patch-plus-channel independent model
+obtains an MSE of .152, compared with .164 without patching \parencite[Table~7]{Nie2023PatchTST}.
+
+This provides positive evidence for patch-based representation learning in the forecasting setting
+under consideration. However, the relevance to industrial classification warrants separate
+investigation, as the prediction target, normalisation, supervision, and aggregation differ.
+
+The exact formulation of PatchTST and its adaptation to the thesis experiments are given in
+Chapter~\ref{ch:foundations}.
+
+Video representation learning looks at another temporal structure. TimeSformer and Video Swin model
+the spatial and temporal relations between video frames \parencite{P034,P013}.
+
+These approaches provide an alternative to simply averaging independently encoded frames. The
+industrial CNN-LSTM remaining useful life study also shows the importance of observation length
+within its simulated prediction task \parencite{AlDulaimi2019A}.
+
+The literature therefore motivates comparisons that explicitly account for sequence length,
+observation windows, temporal ordering, normalisation, pre-training, and fitting budget.
+
+A Temporal Transformer and a compact statistical representation could well be preserving different
+aspects of the same sensor stream. Looking at their individual and combined performance helps assess
+whether the additional representation contains useful information for the task at hand.
+
+\subsection{Audio Representations}
+
+Acoustic representations include MFCCs, log-mel spectra, wavelet maps, GASF transforms and learned
+features.
+
+The Welding Penetration Network uses VMD and GASF; DMAW uses Transformer representations adapted to
+the welding observations \parencite{Hua2026Weld,P003}.
+
+These are alternative forms of acoustic representation. An image-shaped acoustic representation is
+still one derived from sound rather than being an independently acquired camera stream.
+
+The Audio Spectrogram Transformer is a pretrained candidate for acoustic representation learning
+\parencite{P011}. The results on the general audio benchmark are sufficiently promising to warrant
+industrial investigation. However, the appropriateness of such representations will depend heavily
+on the particular acoustic environment and the specific prediction task.
+
+MIMII and ToyADMOS both go some way to establishing the importance of background composition and
+noise environment \parencite{Purohit2019MIMII,Koizumi2019ToyADMOS}.
+
+Together, these studies lead one to consider recording quality, normalisation, and consistent
+observation intervals when looking at audio representations. The REASSEMBLE correspondence checks
+precluded the corresponding predictive comparison in this thesis (Chapter 6).
+
+\subsection{Frozen Representations, Fine-Tuning, and Computational Restrictions}
+
+Freezing an encoder, fitting a small prediction head and then adapting the entire network all
+consume different amounts of statistical and computing resources.
+
+Benchmark methods based on pretrained features show that you can get useful representations without
+going through the entire original representation-learning process on the industrial dataset
+\parencite{Roth2022Towards,Costanzino2024Multimodal}.
+
+The computational cost of pretraining is still part of the representation's provenance even if it's
+external to the downstream experiment.
+
+Frozen feature extraction can therefore enable comparisons under a constrained fitting budget.
+Fine-tuning provides a very different opportunity in that it allows you to adapt the representations
+to the particular target observations. Which of the two is ultimately more effective can only be
+determined by empirical comparison, rather than by assuming one approach is in principle better than
+the other.
+
+Distillation also makes a further distinction between training and deployment. The PGMTKD multimodal
+teachers are used to enrich a pressure-only student. The gated acoustic-vibration study reports a
+distilled LightGBM student, though the nature of the teacher features and the timing of the inputs
+remain ambiguous \parencite{Li2025PGMTKD,Huang2026Rolling}.
+
+Resource comparisons should therefore account for feature extraction, preprocessing, trainable
+components, and any teachers required during inference, rather than just the final prediction model.
+
+For SQ1, these results are sufficient to justify making explicit comparisons of the representation
+and fitting procedures under plausible computing budgets.
+
+\section{Multimodal Fusion Strategies --- SQ2}
+
+Fusion strategies can be broken down according to where the information is fused. Early integration
+fuses raw observations or raw features. Intermediate integration fuses the learned representations.
+Prediction-level fusion fuses the outputs (e.g., class probability estimates, anomaly scores).
+Hybrid architectures use more than one level of integration.
+
+These provide an operational taxonomy for comparing the studies reviewed. Adaptive reweighting
+refers to whether the combination varies according to the observation or its context and can
+therefore operate at different fusion levels.
+
+Terminology from the source papers is kept where appropriate, and comparisons are based on the
+actual location and mechanism of information exchange.
+
+\subsection{Unimodal References and Incremental Modality Value}
+
+The strongest credible single-input predictor serves as the benchmark for investigating SQ2.
+
+The original welding motivation is why this reference is so important. DMAW fuses the image and
+audio Transformer representations to classify the four welding states. The reported accuracy is
+96.1\% with fusion, 90.2\% for the image model and 84.4\% for the audio model \parencite[PDF p.~7,
+Table~III]{P003}.
+
+This supports a predictive benefit from the assessed multimodal pipeline. The grouping of the
+assessment windows by welding run is still not resolved in the evaluation.
+
+In contrast, the weld penetration study reports 100\% clean test accuracy for its image-only,
+audio-only, and fused models \parencite[Tables~1--3]{Hua2026Weld}.
+
+The additional modality therefore provides no measurable gain in accuracy under that comparison.
+
+A similar distinction is made in the case of robotic monitoring. The HANDOVER study reports 69.1\%
+accuracy for video plus force/torque and 67.9\% when gripper state is added to the I3D-D family
+\parencite[PDF p.~7, Table~III]{Thoduka2024Handover}.
+
+These are five-class outcome accuracies averaged over five training runs, rather than binary failure
+average precision. The comparison does, however, show that adding an observation can reduce
+predictive performance.
+
+The tool wear study reports an MAE of 6.6183 for their proposed model, 8.3002 for the visual
+ResNet50 and 60.5118 for the acoustic reference. All the other multimodal architectures evaluated
+have errors in excess of 45 \parencite[PDF p.~24, Table~1]{P045}.
+
+The strong visual reference makes the improvement scientifically informative, though the
+architectural differences mean you cannot attribute all of it to fusion.
+
+Transferable results on the difficulties of multimodal optimisation and on industrial all-channel
+losses underline the need to establish incremental gains against a strong admissible reference
+\parencite{Wang2020What,Goodarzi2023Deep}.
+
+Collectively, these studies support investigating the value of conditional fusion, rather than
+assuming that including additional modalities will necessarily improve prediction.
+
+\subsection{Feature Concatenation}
+
+Feature concatenation combines the representations with no explicit mechanism for adaptive
+allocation and is therefore a key reference point for any more sophisticated form of integration.
+
+Effectiveness depends on feature scale, dimensionality, regularisation, and the quality of the
+constituent representations.
+
+M3DM's direct concatenation ablation is worse than its point cloud reference, while the full hybrid
+system outperforms both individual inputs \parencite[Table~4]{Wang2023Multimodal}.
+
+MADFlow also provides a further protocol-specific comparison between alignment and concatenation
+\parencite{Li2025MADFlow}.
+
+CFA provides transferable evidence from forecasting. For DLinear on the Energy dataset, the first
+concatenation gives an MSE of .670, compared with .263 without text and .243 with constrained fusion
+\parencite[PDF p.~4, Table~4]{P023}.
+
+The constrained fusion method also performs worse in a number of the evaluated scenarios, which
+again suggests that the benefits of an integration mechanism are highly task-dependent.
+
+Manufacturing fusion can similarly improve one architecture at the expense of the other under the
+same broad prediction task \parencite[Table~3]{Li2022A}.
+
+These results establish concatenation as a valid comparator. It should be appropriately normalised
+and fitted, rather than implemented as an artificially weak baseline against which more complex
+methods are compared.
+
+\subsection{Prediction-Level and Late Fusion}
+
+Prediction-level fusion involves fusing the outputs of modality-specific predictors via techniques
+such as averaging, stacking, or evidence aggregation. This is a classification of where the fusion
+occurs; the individual predictors could be trained independently or as part of a wider training
+regime.
+
+Thermal-vibration diagnosis provides direct evidence of physical camera-sensor prediction-level
+fusion. They use separate CNN softmax outputs with weighted Dempster-Shafer aggregation and report a
+clean 100\% diagnostic rate, compared with 99.06\% for IRT and 96.32\% for vibration \parencite[Table~VII]{Huo2022Multisensor}.
+
+The weights were fixed, and the sample-level cross-validation did not establish independence between
+physical runs.
+
+HANDOVER compares late outputs with intermediate sensor integration in the RGB and optical flow
+branches \parencite[PDF pp.~5--7]{Thoduka2024Handover}.
+
+Cross-modal feature mapping and CPIR combine discrepancy maps, which need not be probabilities
+\parencite{Costanzino2024Multimodal,Shangguan2025CPIR}.
+
+Some papers also use the term 'late fusion' to describe representation-level integration prior to
+classification \parencite{Wang2020What}. That underlines the importance of knowing
+what information you are actually fusing.
+
+Stacked generalisation provides a scientific basis for learning a second-stage combination from
+cross-validated predictions. Probability outputs contain predictive information beyond the hard
+class labels so that a second-stage learner can estimate the joint utility of the outputs
+\parencite[Sections~2--3]{Ting1999Issues}.
+
+The referenced study looks at a linear least-squares combiner; this thesis looks at logistic
+stacking. F2 is low-dimensional because it combines two scalar branch probabilities. Its suitability
+still requires task-specific evaluation.
+
+The construction of grouped prediction banks, calibration, and the common outer assessment remain
+key to evaluating the learned combination. The exact form of the F2 and the fitting procedure are
+given in Chapters 3 and 4 respectively.
+
+\subsection{Gated and Context-Conditioned Fusion}
+
+Adaptive fusion is scientifically justified when the utility of the individual inputs varies from
+observation to observation.
+
+Gated Multimodal Units do provide evidence of transferable conditional representation integration.
+The architecture fuses visual and textual information and achieves a macro F-score of .541, compared
+with .521 for the concatenation baseline in the movie-genre classification task \parencite[Section~3.1, Table~2]{P006}.
+
+This finding points to the need to look at conditional allocation, though the mechanism at the
+application and representation level is quite different from the probability- and logit-based gates
+that have been the subject of this thesis.
+
+Adaptive integration has also been looked at in an industrial context. DMAW achieved 96.1\%
+accuracy, compared with 90.5\% for summation and 92.6\% for its gate comparator \parencite[PDF p.~9,
+Table~V]{P003}.
+
+TC-MAF provides another approach whereby the auxiliary RGB evidence is bounded around a multimodal
+anchor using normal training dispersion and local disagreement \parencite[PDF pp.~3--5]{P026}.
+
+These architectures illustrate various approaches to controlling information contribution.
+
+An important evaluation dimension is what governs the allocation. GCCF-AD is described as dynamic
+fusion, but the formulation they report is a learned scalar with no explicit sample-quality input
+\parencite[p.~149226, Eq.~8]{P038}.
+
+PGMTKD uses training losses to assign teacher contributions, and task loss balancing focuses on
+optimisation rather than any direct measure of input quality at deployment
+\parencite{Li2025PGMTKD,P045}.
+
+GAF-Net uses progressive feature feedback, and trustworthy fusion employs evidence-based aggregation
+\parencite{Huang2026Rolling,Ying2025Trustworthy}.
+
+The scientific distinction is what contributions can vary, what information determines that
+variation, and whether that variation improves predictive performance relative to a fixed
+combination.
+
+Process-conditioned weighting provides an additional way to incorporate pertinent context, without
+requiring explicit physical law-constrained modelling.
+
+\subsection{Comparability and Fairness of Fusion Evaluations}
+
+A shared dataset name does not in itself constitute a matched experimental comparison.
+
+Cohort construction, supervision, observation windows, pretraining, feature dimensionality,
+augmentation, and selection exposure could all contribute to differences between architectures.
+
+The tool wear study alters the architectural components as part of the fusion, and the incomplete
+federated imagery study alters the client data access as well as the combining mechanism
+\parencite{P045,P051}.
+
+These are comparative assessments of fitted pipelines. Their interpretation differs from an
+experiment intended to isolate attention, modality contribution, or quality conditioning.
+
+Comparisons using the same evidence sources can support a narrower attribution. TC-MAF retains the
+same evidence sources, comparing bounded fusion against static averaging, weighted scores, rank
+fusion, and a learned gate \parencite[PDF p.~7, Table~3]{P026}.
+
+On the flip side, the disagreement-aware study uses a locally released code baseline that performs
+worse than the published M3DM result \parencite[PDF p.~10, Table~4]{P033}.
+
+Distilled predictors can also access training information that is not available to standard unimodal
+models \parencite{Li2025PGMTKD}.
+
+These settings remain scientifically valuable, provided that the information and fitting conditions
+are clearly specified. Pipeline performance, incremental modality value, and the isolated effect of
+a fusion operator represent distinct evaluation questions.
+
+\section{Reliability and Robustness --- SQ3}
+
+The reliability of a multimodal architecture depends on both the prediction setting and the type of
+intervention acting on its inputs.
+
+Missing channels, corrupted measurements, asynchronous observations and changes in operating
+conditions all represent different reliability issues. The evidence reviewed is therefore grouped by
+the particular condition under consideration rather than assigned to a single general robustness
+category.
+
+\subsection{Missing Modalities}
+
+Planned single-input deployment differs from an unannounced loss of an input in a model that
+normally expects complete observations.
+
+PGMTKD's pressure-only student and incomplete RGB-geometry methods do provide evidence for reduced
+input inference. ModDrop and the missing-modality Transformers add further transferable mechanisms
+and counterexamples \parencite{Li2025PGMTKD,Sui2026Incomplete,P005,P016}.
+
+The effectiveness of these mechanisms will, of course, depend on the training conditions and the
+specific missing-input patterns they have been assessed on.
+
+TC-MAF explicitly evaluates unavailable 3D input. I-AUROC drops from .9788 to .9330 and P-AUPRO from
+.9901 to .9586 \parencite[PDF p.~7, Table~3]{P026}.
+
+This provides a quantified example of performance under a given missing modality condition.
+
+Federated substation monitoring varies the proportion of incomplete clients to 80\% \parencite[Figure~8]{P051}.
+
+Its training and client-availability setting differs from an arbitrary inference-time camera
+failure.
+
+ZSG-IAD reports input subset comparisons without fully defining the shared checkpoint and training
+exposure \parencite{P025}. Missing training images in the hot rolling regime constitute another
+availability regime \parencite{Ma2025A}.
+
+These studies collectively indicate the need to explicitly specify available modalities during both
+training and inference (i.e., availability masks), the policy for replacement or fallback, and the
+system's response when all required inputs are absent.
+
+\subsection{Noise and Partial Degradation}
+
+Physical monitoring studies do provide evidence of a number of controlled degradation mechanisms.
+Thermal-vibration diagnosis examines Gaussian noise and vibration bias/stuck faults, while welding
+penetration recognition examines artificial interference. Acoustic-thermal and acoustic-vibration
+studies look at other noise settings
+\parencite{Huo2022Multisensor,Hua2026Weld,Ying2025Trustworthy,Huang2026Rolling}.
+
+These interventions vary in the observations affected, the degree of corruption and the extent to
+which the model is exposed to degradation.
+
+At $-2$~dB, noisy IR thermography gives 95.26\%, which jumps to 97.6\% when combined with fault-free
+vibration. Noisy vibration alone gives 36.86\%, jumping to 94.2\% when combined with fault-free IR
+thermography \parencite[Table~VIII]{Huo2022Multisensor}.
+
+The comparison shows the value of a fault-free companion modality when another observation is
+synthetically corrupted.
+
+The welding penetration study reports a fused accuracy of 93.33\% under its interference condition
+\parencite[Table~3]{Hua2026Weld}.
+
+The corresponding corrupted unimodal comparisons are required in order to assess the incremental
+benefit of fusion in the interference condition. The reported intervention also leaves some
+uncertainty about the corruption operator, magnitude, and exposure matching.
+
+Synthetic signal-to-noise interventions provide some controlled experimental evidence, but the
+conclusions are specific to the observations affected and the severities tested. Natural
+simultaneous degradation and ordinary defective objects are quite different phenomena.
+
+These distinctions lead directly to a comparison of predictions from degraded input against those
+from static fusion and single-input predictors under the same reliability condition.
+
+\subsection{Temporal Misalignment and Distribution Shift}
+
+Temporal misalignment and distribution shift both affect multimodal prediction, albeit in different
+ways.
+
+A clock offset can break correspondence between observations, whereas a distribution shift can
+change the conditions under which the learned representation or predictor is evaluated.
+
+Registration studies have established the importance of alignment in multimodal monitoring.
+OmniFuser's repeated within-pass images provide a way to construct paired observations rather than
+directly test a synchronisation failure \parencite{Petrich2021Multimodal,Chen2023Multisensor,P021}.
+
+The literature also contains examples of evaluation under changed acquisition conditions. HANDOVER
+trains on one robot and evaluates on a different robot, and there's a rail localisation study that
+evaluates a Subway-trained model on high-speed rail \parencite[PDF p.~7, Table~V]{Thoduka2024Handover};
+\parencite[PDF p.~10, Table~III]{P031}.
+
+Target-adaptive methods, target-validated condition studies and independent build experiments are
+all different forms of access to the assessment domain
+\parencite{Wang2025A,Chu2024A,Huang2026Rolling,Bevans2023Heterogeneous}.
+
+Rebuilding normal memories on Eyecandies using fixed fusion hyperparameters is evidence of recipe
+transfer, rather than deploying an unaltered model to an untouched target installation
+\parencite{P026,P033}.
+
+These distinctions between them are important for interpreting the external dataset work in this
+thesis. Architecture reuse, representation transfer, temporal offset tolerance, and unchanged model
+generalisation each require distinct evaluation protocols.
+
+\subsection{Quality-Aware and Uncertainty-Aware Fusion}
+
+Quality-aware fusion is driven by the idea that the predictive utility of an input may vary with its
+quality. Signal quality, model confidence, calibrated probability and evidence consistency are all
+different beasts, though.
+
+QMF furnishes a theoretical basis for studying sample-dependent weighting via generalisation upper
+bounds under stated relationships between uncertainty and loss, assuming a common training and test
+distribution \parencite[Sections~3.1--3.2]{Zhang2023QMF}.
+
+The transferable UDML experiments train on controlled Gaussian corruption intensities and recognise
+that sample difficulty can be confused with modality corruption \parencite[PDF pp.~4--5, 9]{P022}.
+
+These results lead us to ask whether the quality information we have available helps us identify
+when a particular observation is no longer useful.
+
+QMF reports an NYU scene classification accuracy of 70.09\% on clean inputs, compared to 70.30\% for
+concatenation. At corruption intensity ten, it reports 55.60\% compared to 51.99\% for late fusion
+\parencite[PDF p.~8, Table~1]{Zhang2023QMF}.
+
+These non-industrial RGB-depth comparisons do show a regime-specific advantage under corruption. The
+clean and corrupted comparisons quoted use different reference methods, so they should not be read
+as a clean-versus-corrupted trade-off against the same reference method.
+
+The effect of the adaptation mechanisms also differs across prediction endpoints. The disagreement
+add-on increases the local MVTec image AUROC from .779 to .800 and, at the same time, reduces the
+Eyecandies image AUROC from .810 to .794, even though the pixel metrics have improved \parencite[PDF
+pp.~10--11, Tables~4--5]{P033}.
+
+TC-MAF uses normal-training dispersion and local disagreement to control the contribution of
+auxiliary evidence \parencite{P026}.
+
+Collectively, these findings establish a scientific basis for reducing reliance on a stream when the
+observable context is expected to indicate errors. They also underscore the need to assess the
+relationship between available quality indicators and actual predictive reliability.
+
+QMF's theoretical justification does not validate the use of arbitrary quality proxies, and clean
+calibration can actually degrade after a distribution shift \parencite{Zhang2023QMF,P008}.
+
+For the empirical investigation, the relevant comparison is whether quality-conditioned weighting
+improves performance over a static model with the same level of corruption exposure, while still
+maintaining an acceptable level of clean performance.
+
+\subsection{Modality Dropout, Augmentation, and Fallback Mechanisms}
+
+Different robustness mechanisms deal with different aspects of input availability.
+
+Training-time modality dropout exposes the model to certain absence patterns. Imputation produces
+substitute observations, and deterministic fallbacks route the prediction to an available unimodal
+component.
+
+These approaches can generate similar outputs under certain conditions without relying on the same
+learned recovery mechanism.
+
+Fuzzy regularisation works by introducing failures via partial corruption and by replacing the
+entire input with a constant or noise. The evaluation is of Gazebo pose regression using random
+sample splits of overlapping windows \parencite[PDF pp.~3--5]{Altinses2023Fuzzy}.
+
+The investigation provides evidence for controlled input regularisation, with independent physical
+deployment outage performance remaining unaddressed.
+
+OmniFuser describes dropout and masking without any quantified outage table \parencite[PDF p.~10]{P021}.
+
+Generated replacement observations can also have a negative impact on performance. Replacing the
+clean depth with CycleGAN output worsens the synthetic robot benchmark's MSE (from $2.1745 \times
+10^{-3}$ to $13.472 \times 10^{-3}$) \parencite[Table~7]{Altinses2025Performance}.
+
+These results motivate individual evaluation of learned recovery, training exposure, and explicit
+fallback policies. Static controls, with equivalent degradation exposure and predictions from the
+surviving input, provide a useful baseline for assessing adaptive robustness.
+
+\section{Evaluation Beyond Accuracy --- SQ4}
+
+The practical value of a monitoring architecture will depend entirely on how its predictions are
+evaluated, chosen, and interpreted.
+
+Accuracy in prediction can be affected by class distribution, assessment grouping and decision
+thresholds. A single aggregate score therefore only provides one facet of the evidence needed to
+assess the performance of a multimodal industrial monitoring system.
+
+SQ4 looks at assessment independence, discrimination, calibration, uncertainty, interpretability,
+computational viability and reproducibility.
+
+\subsection{Grouped Evaluation and Leakage Prevention}
+
+The independent assessment unit may be a weld, a tool, a participant, a recording, a machine or a
+build.
+
+Randomly partitioned observations can end up with shared acquisition contextual information or raw
+samples. The fuzzy-regularisation study forms 20-step windows with a stride of one and then randomly
+divides the resulting examples \parencite[PDF pp.~4--5]{Altinses2023Fuzzy}.
+
+Its assessment therefore doesn't establish performance on unseen trajectories.
+
+Weld run grouping is still unresolved in the closest welding studies \parencite{P003,Hua2026Weld}. These
+examples do show a potential dependence between the observations but do not establish any particular
+level of leakage.
+
+The HANDOVER authors report an explicit participant-disjoint official split. An adapted learned
+fusion implementation would still require participant information in order to build the
+corresponding inner partitions \parencite{Thoduka2024Handover}.
+
+Normalisation, feature selection and model parameter optimisation all require an appropriate split
+between model development and model assessment \parencite{P004,P017}.
+
+Out-of-fold stacking provides a mechanism for constructing second-stage prediction inputs without
+any in-sample predictions. Its validity is of course also dependent on the way in which the
+underlying fitting, selection and calibration folds are constructed.
+
+Repeatedly selecting follow-up experiments after looking at the assessment results introduces
+further selection bias, even if the individual model fits would have ruled out their outer test
+labels.
+
+These results motivate the development of assessment protocols that account for the physical origin
+and dependence of observations throughout the modelling process.
+
+\subsection{Discrimination, Calibration, and Decision Thresholds}
+
+Ranking, probability quality and thresholded decisions are three different aspects of a predictor.
+
+AUROC measures discrimination across thresholds rather than performance at a single operational
+false alarm rate or the quality of the predicted probabilities.
+
+MVTec AD shows that useful ranking can exist alongside fairly poor thresholded localisation. The
+strict FPR metrics highlight a different aspect of detection performance
+\parencite{Bergmann2019MVTec,Costanzino2024Multimodal,Cheng2025Multimodal}.
+
+The rail vision-language study reports pixel-level metrics and optimal threshold F1 \parencite[PDF
+p.~7]{P031}. These metrics address a completely different evaluation problem than image-level alarm
+accuracy at a threshold chosen from the training or validation set.
+
+In manufacturing, \textcite{Petrich2021Multimodal} report an accuracy of .9921 and a precision of .2487 on an
+imbalanced layer, which is a clear example of the impact of class distribution and threshold
+selection.
+
+Validation-fitted calibration can improve in-distribution probability quality, while dataset shift
+can damage confidence estimates \parencite{Guo2017Calibration,P008}.
+
+Proper scoring rules (negative log-likelihood, Brier score, etc.) provide a basis for assessing
+probability forecasts \parencite{P055}. Their interpretation differs from that of discrimination or
+thresholded classification.
+
+A generated confidence field or evidence mass cannot replace probability evaluation. Metrics should
+align with the prediction target and class distribution, and operational thresholds should be
+selected using admissible training or validation observations.
+
+\subsection{Uncertainty and Statistical Comparisons}
+
+Seed variation, acquisition group sampling and model selection uncertainty are all different sources
+of variation.
+
+HANDOVER's reported standard deviations are for five training runs on a fixed test set, whereas a
+group bootstrap of saved predictions samples assessment units conditional on the fitted models
+\parencite{Thoduka2024Handover}.
+
+These approaches capture different facets of uncertainty and do not necessarily encompass all the
+variation expected from retraining or deployment in a different domain.
+
+Cross-validation folds share training observations. Under the assumptions stated, the classic
+variance result shows that no universally unbiased estimator of cross-validation variance exists;
+ignoring covariance can substantially underestimate uncertainty \parencite[pp.~1096--1098, 1102--1104]{Bengio2004CVVariance}. Conditional uncertainty intervals can still be useful when the
+estimand and dependence assumptions are stated explicitly.
+
+Small fusion effects, ambiguous repetition counts and conflicting descriptions of significance all
+warrant the same level of caution \parencite{Gu2024Improved,Xu2025ModalityResilient}.
+
+Paired performance differences on common assessment observations provide a much more direct
+comparison than unrelated score intervals. An interval which contains zero doesn't demonstrate
+equivalence, and a positive point estimate doesn't establish practical importance without an
+appropriate criterion.
+
+\subsection{Interpretability and Its Limits}
+
+Prediction scores, coefficients, gate traces and spatial maps can all tell you something regarding
+how the model is behaving.
+
+The attention debate has both counterexamples to faithful explanation and evidence that the learned
+attention weights do, in fact, contain useful information. This leads to the conclusion that
+interpretability should be evaluated on a task-specific basis rather than drawing general
+conclusions from the terminology of the architecture itself \parencite{P036}.
+
+ZSG-IAD measures schema validity, language consistency and coarse-to-final-mask overlap
+\parencite{P025}. These provide evidence of various forms of self-consistency.
+
+Their interpretation differs from both independent physical defect causation and calibrated
+confidence.
+
+HANDOVER leaves the use of human action prediction as a means of explanation as a future area of
+research \parencite{Thoduka2024Handover}.
+
+Within this thesis, observable quality responses and inspectable fusion coefficients provide
+descriptive transparency. However, expert understanding, causal attribution, and operator usability
+require additional assessments.
+
+Process context should be distinguished from the explicit physical equation constraints (e.g.
+PGMTKD's pressure model \parencite{Li2025PGMTKD}).
+
+\subsection{Modularity, Reproducibility, and Computational Cost}
+
+Modular architectures enable you to swap in and out individual representations and prediction
+components and compare them. Their value for scientific investigation comes from the fact that you
+can identify which component is different between conditions.
+
+Modularity does not establish predictive robustness or an advantage over a matched end-to-end
+architecture.
+
+An identified version, accessible source code, executable processing and independently reproduced
+results all provide various levels of reproducibility assurance \parencite{P009}.
+
+The difference between local and published M3DM results remains unresolved. It motivates checks of
+dependencies, valid point filtering, checkpoints, and scoring conventions \parencite{P033}.
+
+Computational comparisons of course also require a common measurement boundary. The CPIR paper
+reports lower FPS than CFM, which is a clear performance vs speed trade-off
+\parencite{Shangguan2025CPIR}.
+
+The reported 5.65~ms for the lightweight image network doesn't include the independent sensor branch
+or acquisition delay \parencite[p.~6661, Table~V]{P035}.
+
+TimeSformer's compute requirements depend on the ImageNet pretraining and the workload it is
+evaluated on \parencite{P034}.
+
+Hardware, batch size, caching, synchronisation and the processing stages included all determine
+which computational measurements can be compared.
+
+These results indicate the necessity for explicit resource accounting and reproducibility
+documentation. Prospective deployment and operator usability remain open questions for validating a
+successful offline forward pass.
+
+\section{Critical Synthesis and Research Gap}
+
+The reviewed literature shows that multimodal observations and learned representations can benefit
+industrial and robotic monitoring. Their value, however, depends on the prediction task, the
+information each representation contains, and the conditions under which the integration method is
+evaluated.
+
+The previous sections have identified four related scientific questions: which representations are
+appropriate for a given target, whether combining their predictions is valuable, whether that value
+is maintained as observation quality degrades, and how the experimental assessments should be
+conducted.
+
+This section synthesises existing findings and outstanding scientific questions to establish design
+requirements for the empirical work.
+
+\subsection{Findings Supported Across the Reviewed Studies}
+
+There are tangible observations that can be used to improve the performance of a task-specific
+monitoring pipeline. DMAW, thermal-vibration diagnosis, HANDOVER and tool wear prediction are all
+good examples under their respective assessment protocols
+\parencite{P003,Huo2022Multisensor,Thoduka2024Handover,P045}.
+
+Controlled evidence integration can also improve RGB--geometry inspection under intact-input
+conditions. TC-MAF separately reports performance when the 3D input is unavailable \parencite{P026}.
+
+The literature therefore establishes that useful industrial multimodal fusion mechanisms and
+approaches to dealing with incomplete observations do in fact exist.
+
+Representation learning offers a number of scientifically sound options for extracting information
+relevant to the task. Pretrained convolutional features, patch-based temporal representations, and
+hand-crafted features are all candidates with some supporting evidence under certain learning and
+evaluation regimes \parencite{Roth2022Towards,Nie2023PatchTST,Horwitz2023Back}.
+
+Their suitability for another industrial prediction target will, of course, depend on several
+factors, including the observations, the supervision, the aggregation, and the compute resources
+involved.
+
+Static and learned fusion mechanisms both offer different avenues for exploiting complementary
+information. Stacked generalisation motivates learning combinations of the outputs of individual
+predictors. In contrast, gated representation learning and quality-aware fusion justify
+context-dependent allocation \parencite{Ting1999Issues,P006,Zhang2023QMF}.
+
+A further well-established distinction concerns input reliability. Planned single-input deployment,
+complete inference-time absence, controlled corruption, and target domain adaptation all address
+different issues \parencite{Li2025PGMTKD,Altinses2023Fuzzy,P051}.
+
+The consistent finding across the reviewed studies is a conditional benefit under specific tasks and
+evaluation regimes, rather than universal superiority of any single representation or fusion
+architecture.
+
+\subsection{Unresolved Questions and Research Gap}
+
+The evidence reviewed also contains significant negative and, indeed, conflicting findings.
+
+The same clean-test accuracy is observed for the unimodal and fused welding models. HANDOVER
+performance drops when the gripper state is added. Some alternative multimodal tool-wear models
+perform worse than the visual reference, although the proposed model improves on it. The Eyecandies
+image scores also go down. All of this calls into question the notion of an unqualified fusion
+benefit \parencite{Hua2026Weld,Thoduka2024Handover,P045,P033}.
+
+Category-specific geometry losses, poor hydraulic channel combinations, and the difficulties of
+multimodal optimisation all reinforce this conclusion
+\parencite{Horwitz2023Back,Bonfiglioli2023The,Goodarzi2023Deep,Wang2020What}.
+
+Several mechanisms could account for these results: redundancy, inappropriate representations,
+optimisation difficulties, noise, supervision and limited independent sample support. The studies do
+not point to a single underlying explanation.
+
+An important unresolved issue is the distinction between a representation's standalone strength and
+its complementary predictive value. A representation that is weak in isolation may still provide
+information useful for another predictor. Conversely, a strong additional representation does not
+guarantee that a fusion mechanism can effectively utilise that information.
+
+Reliability introduces a related source of uncertainty. Adaptive weighting is justified when the
+available context can identify changes in unimodal prediction reliability \parencite{Zhang2023QMF,P022}.
+The value of conditioning information, its relationship to branch errors, and its comparison with
+exposure-matched static alternatives must be investigated on a task-specific basis.
+
+External evaluation adds another boundary. Re-using a procedure with a new dataset-specific fitting,
+re-using the learned representation weights and then evaluating an unaltered predictor in a
+different domain are all different experimental questions. You cannot just take the results of one
+and treat them as equivalent evidence of generalisation.
+
+The following table summarises these unresolved relationships across the principal evaluation axes
+of the review. It complements the detailed work-level comparison matrix in
+Appendix~\ref{app:literature}.
+
